WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:13.160 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  since the dawn of gaming until the  current day  gamers and game developers alike have  built up a unique lingo to communicate  terminology which describes certain  aspects of game design and mechanics  play styles or in-game objects and often  transcends different cultures and  languages  this kind of terminology is something  people have simply agreed upon over the  many years  sometimes suggested by the game  developers themselves other times by the  community devoted to said games  one such term which most gamers are all  too familiar with  is the dungeon it's a name used to  describe an enclosed maze-like  area commonly filled with puzzles deadly  traps and combat challenges in the form  of hostile npcs  and often times a boss at the end they  are commonly found in rpgs and  action-adventure games  and depending on the genre the goal of a  dungeon may vary but  generally speaking it's a place where  some of the best loot and weapons are  found a great place to grind and in  story-driven games often mandatory to  advance the game's plot the act of  venturing into these dark caverns  treacherous keeps and complex mazes  either alone or with a party is referred  to as a dungeon crawl  like a pub crawl except without beer or  monsters  if you're lucky there's even an entire  genre of games devoted to this concept  alone  like diablo the binding of isaac hades  minecraft dungeons and many more  the word dungeon 2 is just another one  of those names people have agreed upon  even when the game in question doesn't  explicitly refer to it as such  as an example the very first zelda game  from 1986  never refers to its underground  challenges as dungeons specifically  in game they are labeled as levels and  in the manual they are referred to as  labyrinths and yet most people even back  in the day still opted to use the term  dungeon instead the second game in the  franchise zelda 2 the adventure of link  called them palaces  it wasn't until a link to the past that  we find the first use of the word  dungeon in the game's manual to describe  the various temples  caves and keeps link has to overcome and  nowadays the devs working on the zelda  games have fully adopted the term  that being said why do we call them  dungeons  why not trials or mazes or anything else  really this  is a valid question to ask since the  real life equivalent of a dungeon has  little to no correlation with the ones  we encounter in video games  so where did the term come from who  coined it and when did it come to  represent that which we find in modern  video games today  [Music]  the word dungeon actually has quite a  bit of history and misconceptions behind  it  originating from the french word don  john it commonly refers to a room or  cell in which prisoners are held  particularly on the ground however the  original french word actually translates  to something very different  namely keep which is the main tower of a  castle these towers are among the most  fortified parts of the castle and were  used as a refuge of last resort should  the rest of the castle fall to  an adversary and not necessarily for  housing prisoners  in english the definition of a dungeon  seems to have been mixed up with that of  another french word  obliet based on oblie meaning to forget  an obliet is more in line with that  which the english dungeon refers to  a basement room with only one way in and  out usually through a hatch in the  ceiling  initially people assumed that these  underground rooms were used to house  prisoners and were even associated with  torture  but even this turned out to be a  misconception nowadays archaeologists  and scholars of medieval architecture  have since agreed that these  underground basements were primarily  used as a storage space and nothing more  of course this isn't a very exciting  story to tell to a tourist  hence why in some cases tour guides  further perpetuate this misunderstanding  in order to romanticize medieval ruins  this isn't to say that medieval prisons  didn't exist but they were definitely  not as macabre and creepy as the ones we  see in classic fantasy stories  these are in fact pure fantasy and  fiction and were built on the  misconceptions about the middle ages by  18th century scholars and further  perpetuated by novelists in the decades  after  as such the word dungeon evolved to  include prison cells and torture  chambers  giving shape to the classic dark  underground cage-filled room of horrors  as we know them today  obliets and dungeons were a favorite  topic of 19th century gothic novels  where they appeared as symbols of hidden  cruelty or tyrannical power  usually found under medieval castles or  abbeys they were used by villainous  characters to hold their adversaries  prisoner  as soon as film and television made  their debut it adapted the imagery of  the dark prison-like dungeon as  described by fantasy novels to the big  screen  and thus it became a main staple in both  horror and fantasy media it started with  silent films from the 20s and 30s and  even cartoons weighed in on the action  they would show dark medieval-like  environments sometimes even with  treasure laying around in the background  this image of the classic fantasy  dungeon continued all the way into the  60s and 70s  most famously with many of the hammer  films like dracula and frankenstein but  aside from the atmosphere and gothic  aesthetics they still hold little to no  resemblance to that of video game  dungeons as we know them today they are  usually described or depicted as  a singular room with the sole purpose of  locking away unfortunate souls there are  no traps to be found or puzzles to solve  or monsters to fight  the sudden leap from a single room  prison to a challenging  complex labyrinth finds its origins in  the rpg genre  and when i say rpg i don't mean final  fantasy  dragon quests or even the classics like  diablo or ultima  in fact the rpgs we're talking about  aren't even video games  instead we're talking about board games  more specifically  tabletop fantasy role-playing games the  most famous of which  of course being dungeons and dragons  also referred to as dnd  it's debatable whether or not dnd was  the very first of its  kind there was a game named dungeon  exclamation mark which released a year  after dnd in 1975 but is said to have  had a prototype ready as early as 1972.  despite this dnd's publication in 1974  is commonly recognized as the beginning  of modern day role-playing games and the  role-playing game industry  its overall themes are influenced by  world mythology history pulp fiction and  contemporary fantasy novels like lord of  the rings its rules and systems are a  unique blend of different sources  deriving from miniature war games  theater and cinema and various fantasy  novels for the rules and restrictions  involving magic and spells  as is with many board games dnd is not  meant to be played alone the game has a  minimum requirement of two players  though  generally speaking three to six players  is considered to be optimal to provide  the best experience  dnd leans heavily on imagination and can  even be played without the use of pawns  miniatures or even a board or map  although the end goal of an individual  session can vary the game is primarily  centered around traversing an imaginary  world or dungeon  fighting monsters and collecting  treasures one of the defining traits of  dnd is the inclusion of the dungeon  master  an individual who determines the rules  the layout of the environment  monster types locations of treasure and  goals for the other players to  accomplish  essentially the dungeon master is the  equivalent of a video game level  designer as well as acting as a  storyteller or narrator for the  adventure at hand the game set the  standard for many elements we  find in rpgs nowadays things like hit  points and experience points  aka hp and xp character classes  character stats  the list goes on and as such it also  brought the concept of a dungeon as a  labyrinthian challenge rather than a  single room prison into modern day pop  culture  which brings us to video games games did  exist even before d d's release  most notably pong from 1970 which is  considered as the first  commercially successful video game and  the magnavox odyssey the very first  commercially available video game  console ever made  released in 1972 but the medium was  still very much in its infancy around  this time  early video games were mostly based on  physics like tennis games bullets and  asteroids as the popularity of dnd  caught on it didn't take developers long  to realize that the rules and logic of  dnd could easily be translated to actual  code since it was mostly based on  numbers and variables  the earliest video game adaptation of  dungeons and dragons is  again debatable though many point to  pettit 5  as one of if not the first  computer-based dungeon crawler inspired  by the dnd formula  there are talks about a similar game  called m199h  which was said to have existed even  earlier but unfortunately there are no  copies of the game's code in existence  to verify it the story of pettit 5 is  quite interesting  and as you will soon find out  single-handedly created a snowball  effect which led to many of the famous  rpgs we know today  it saw the light of day in 1975 only a  year after dnd's debut and it ran on  plato  an operating system used at the  university of illinois its creator  reginald quote unquote rusty ruthford  was a member of the population and  energy group one of several groups  active at the university each group was  assigned a limited number of program  slots on the school's mainframe which  they were allowed to utilize  the population and energy group had five  which were all named with the prefix  pedit followed by the number  the first three slots were already in  use so rutford used the fifth to code  his game and the fourth for the game's  manual  it uses the basic features of dnd as  much as possible  hit points monster levels experience and  treasure awards and so on the game was  never commercially available and  to make matters worse the university  administrators would often delete it  from the system to save up a memory  inevitably this led to rutford  abandoning the project however it had  become so popular within the university  that others began to take the game's  code and made their own versions this  spawned an entire generation of dnd  inspired games based on pettit 5's core  but with all kinds of revisions  changes and improvements these early  games collectively formed the boundaries  within which  games would develop as video games  entered the 80s  the genre bloomed giving rise to many  dungeon-based rpgs and action-adventure  games like wizardry  ultima the bart's tale mighty magic  megami tensei fantasy star  dungeon master gauntlet and many more  who all helped set the standards for the  genre in the decades to come  both on home computers consoles and even  the arcade  even dnd itself was translated to video  game format multiple times and even a  new installment dungeons and dragons  dark alliance is set for release in 2021  thus we arrive at the current day going  back to  familiar territory for a moment it's  understandable why nintendo initially  didn't use the term dungeon to describe  their underground challenges in the  zelda games  instead calling them levels labyrinths  or palaces zelda wasn't an rpg  after all but although different players  quickly noticed all the similarities to  many other games of its era  self-contained stages separate from the  overworld a place where you primarily  solve puzzles fight monsters and bosses  obtain weapons and items and better  armor even though there are no  experience points involved no party  members  character classes or jobs in terms of  atmosphere and mechanics they are so  similar that players simply started  referring to them as dungeons either way  and nintendo was quick to adapt and  follow the trend set by its fanbase  as the word dungeon became a main staple  in gaming obviously the use of the term  became more and more lenient  nowadays it no longer refers to just a  gothic medieval-style  prison-like maze made of cobblestone  iron bars bones and skeletons they can  represent all sorts of different types  of architecture  atmosphere and aesthetics like caves  mines ancient temples crypts floating  cities  you name it although a more broad  concept nowadays  it's an interesting realization that  something so familiar in video games  which is considered a modern day  invention  finds its origin so far back in time  going from medieval architecture to  early 19th century gothic fantasy novels  to early television to a tabletop board  game which  in turn inspired multiple genres of  video games in the decades after  hey thank you for watching if you liked  this video then  good good good good that makes me happy  what did you expect me to say leave a  like and subscribe  nonsense i don't play by those rules  anyway a big thanks to my patreons and  members who continue to make these  videos possible  also please welcome heist it co-co  laptop account  bedo alexander tash as well as the  return of one of my og patreons  julian clement thank you all so much for  your amazing support  i will see you all next time this is  dawn signing off and okay do please like  and subscribe it really helps  [Music]

